From 4e3bb2f4526c82621212ef291f571fa277e08b37 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Mar 2026 02:37:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] client/wayland: Forward key events via virtual keyboard in
 IMv2

The IMv2 protocol has no keysym request, so forward_key_event from
IBus engines was silently dropped with a g_warning("TODO").

When the engine provides a nonzero keycode (the evdev scancode it
originally received), forward it via ibus_wayland_im_key() which
sends it through the virtual keyboard.  This covers the common case
of engines forwarding keys like Enter, Escape, and BackSpace.

Engines that synthesize key events with keycode=0 still fall through
to the unimplemented keysym path, which would require a
keysym-to-keycode reverse lookup via xkb_keymap_key_for_each().

BUG=https://github.com/ibus/ibus/pull/2887
---
 client/wayland/ibuswaylandim.c | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/client/wayland/ibuswaylandim.c b/client/wayland/ibuswaylandim.c
index d4fd2db84..85581fcb2 100644
--- a/client/wayland/ibuswaylandim.c
+++ b/client/wayland/ibuswaylandim.c
@@ -432,6 +432,22 @@ _context_forward_key_event_cb (IBusInputContext *context,
     else
         state = WL_KEYBOARD_KEY_STATE_PRESSED;
 
+    /* IMv2 has no keysym request; the only way to forward a key is via
+     * the virtual keyboard, which takes an evdev keycode.  Use the
+     * keycode from the engine when available.  Engines that synthesize
+     * key events without a real keycode pass keycode=0; for those we
+     * fall through to ibus_wayland_im_keysym() which logs a warning
+     * in IMv2 (keysym-to-keycode reverse lookup is not yet implemented).
+     */
+    if (priv->version == INPUT_METHOD_V2 && keycode != 0) {
+        ibus_wayland_im_key (wlim,
+                             priv->im_serial,
+                             0,
+                             keycode,
+                             state);
+        return;
+    }
+
     ibus_wayland_im_keysym (wlim,
                             priv->im_serial,
                             keyval,
